    "updated_at": "2026-09-02T18:50:26",
    "reason": "status=OKで確定しました。",
    "final_status": "OK",
    "wav_path": "er006_output/pool_pilot_01/pool_n18_notifications_specfix_v2/b1b/narration/full_story_part1.wav",
    "cumulative_tts_attempts": 1,
    "cumulative_asr_calls": 1,
    "budget_guard_triggered": false,
    "last_attempts_log": [
      {
        "attempt": 1,
        "status": "OK",
        "asr_text": "Imagine you are working. Your phone makes a sound. You do not pick it up or even look at the screen. Still, the moment feels different. Research suggests that distraction may begin before you check. Two peer-reviewed studies help explain why. In a 2022 study, 73 university students completed a visual task while either a notification sound or an unrelated comparison sound played. They did not look at or touch a phone, even so, the sound affected how they handled the task. A 2023 study examined a quieter situation. 42 university students took a concentration test with a powered-off smartphone on the desk or in another room. No notifications were received. This study also found a difference between the two settings.",
        "instruction_type": "english_style_prefix_wide_margin",
        "audio_classification": "NORMALIZED_MATCH",
        "length_ok": true,
        "verified": true,
        "trim_info": {
          "raw_duration_seconds": 48.931,
          "raw_leading_silence_seconds": 0.26,
          "raw_trailing_silence_seconds": 0.26,
          "trimmed_duration_seconds": 48.931,
          "leading_margin_retained_seconds": 0.26,
          "trailing_margin_retained_seconds": 0.26
        },
        "disfluency_checked": false,
        "disfluency_evidence": null
      }
    ]
  },
  "full_story_part2": {
    "state": "RESOLVED",
    "canonical_text_sha256": "7b51ee482025c1f5dd8c0cbccdfdbcb2d9fe98ff700eeef80abbeceea9cef8ab",
    "language": "en",
    "theme_id": "pool_n18_notifications_specfix_v2",
    "level": "b1b",
    "segment_id": "full_story_part2",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-02T18:51:20",
    "reason": "status=OKで確定しました。",
    "final_status": "OK",
    "wav_path": "er006_output/pool_pilot_01/pool_n18_notifications_specfix_v2/b1b/narration/full_story_part2.wav",
    "cumulative_tts_attempts": 1,
    "cumulative_asr_calls": 1,
    "budget_guard_triggered": false,
    "last_attempts_log": [
      {
        "attempt": 1,
        "status": "OK",
        "asr_text": "At first, this seems like a story about willpower, but the studies point to two separate pressures. One comes from the sound, the other comes from the device being part of the workspace. Together, they suggest that not checking is not the same as not being affected. The studies used specific tasks and university students, so they do not show that every phone affects every person in every situation. Outside the lab, this pull is also part of daily life for many U.S. teens. In a 2018 survey of teens aged 13 to 17, 72% said they often or sometimes checked messages or notifications soon after waking. When their phone was not with them, 56% reported feeling anxious, lonely, upset, or some combination of these. A majority also said they felt a need to reply quickly to texts, social media messages, or other notifications.",
        "instruction_type": "english_style_prefix_wide_margin",
        "audio_classification": "NORMALIZED_MATCH",
        "length_ok": true,
        "verified": true,
        "trim_info": {
          "raw_duration_seconds": 64.211,
          "raw_leading_silence_seconds": 0.04,
          "raw_trailing_silence_seconds": 0.04,
          "trimmed_duration_seconds": 64.211,
          "leading_margin_retained_seconds": 0.04,
          "trailing_margin_retained_seconds": 0.04
        },
        "disfluency_checked": false,
